    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having an issue with some walnut I'm working with for a cabinet project. I'm cutting dovetails by hand, but I keep getting these small splinters and tear-outs right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the fit. Any idea what I might be doing wrong with my chisel or my grain direction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are likely pushing the chisel too deep into the wood at the tip, tearing off the wood fibers before the chisel angle takes hold properly. Ensure your grain slopes downward toward the chisel, and avoid forcing the tool; instead, make many light passes with the flat edge of the chisel to remove the wood cleanly. A干净整洁的切面通常取决于下刀柔和，以及下刀时保持稳定的垂直角度。\n</think>\n\nYou are likely pushing the chisel too deep into the wood at the tip before it fully engages, which causes the tear-out. Ensure the wood grain slopes downwards away from the cutting edge of your chisel to support the fibers. Making many light, controlled passes with the flat side of the chisel will often improve the finish and improve the fit.",
      "source": "target",
