    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, I'll try lighter passes. I have been pushing pretty hard to get through the end grain though. Should I be sharpening my chisels to a specific angle to help with the splintering, or is it more about the moisture in the walnut itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Actually, hard grain like walnut often creates its own natural reinforcement called \"tang,\" so it's not usually a moisture issue but rather the angle of the blade that matters. You should sharpen your chisels to a slightly sharper, more acute angle than what is typically recommended for soft woods. This sharper bevel will slice through the hard walnut fibers more cleanly rather than crushing them into tear-outs.",
      "source": "target",
